        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/problem": "Find $g^{\\prime\\prime}(4)$ if $g(x)=8x^{2}\\sqrt{x}-2x+3.$\n\n{{selection}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g^{\\prime}(x) &= \\left(8x^{2}\\sqrt{x}-2x+3\\right)^{\\prime} \\\\ &= \\left(8x^{5/2}-2x+3\\right)^{\\prime} \\\\ &= (8)\\cdot(\\frac{5}{2})\\cdot x^{3/2}+(-2)\\cdot(1)\\cdot x^{0}+0 \\\\ &= 20 x^{\\frac{3}{2}} - 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g^{\\prime\\prime}(x) &= \\left(20 x^{\\frac{3}{2}} - 2\\right)^{\\prime} \\\\ &= (20)\\cdot(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot x^{1/2}+0 \\\\ &= 30 \\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the second derivative at $x=4$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g^{\\prime\\prime}(4) &= 30 \\sqrt{(4)} \\\\ &= 60.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
